## Indicating success of an invoked [=native payment app=] ## {#sctn-indicating-success}

A [=native payment app provider=] MUST provide a way for an invoked [=native
payment app=] to <dfn>indicate success</dfn> back to the user agent. When this
occurs, the user agent MUST run the [=user accepts the payment request
algorithm=] from [[payment-request]].

A [=native payment app provider=] MUST provide a way for the user agent to
<dfn>retrieve the converted result details</dfn> for an invoked [=native
payment app=] that has [=indicated success=]. The details must be converted to
a form suitable for the user agent to place in the {{PaymentResponse/details}}
field of the response that will be returned from {{PaymentRequest/show()}}.

TODO: Actually because we need to return shipping address/etc here too, we need
    to specify a new NativePaymentHandlerResponse object, have this method
    return one of those, and use that in the steps to respond to a payment
    request.

## Indicating user cancel from an invoked [=native payment app=] ## {#sctn-indicating-user-cancel}

A [=native payment app provider=] MUST provide a way for an invoked [=native
payment app=] to <dfn>indicate user cancellation</dfn> back to the user agent.
When this occurs, the user agent MUST run the [=user aborts the payment request
algorithm=] from [[payment-request]].

## Indicating internal error from an invoked [=native payment app=] ## {#sctn-indicating-internal-error}

A [=native payment app provider=] MUST provide a way for an invoked [=native
payment app=] to <dfn>indicate internal error</dfn> back to the user agent.
When this occurs, the user agent MUST run the [=payment handler indicates an
internal error algorithm=] from [[payment-request]].

<div class="non-normative">

# Security and privacy considerations # {#sctn-security-and-privacy}

## Tracking risks via readiness checks ## {#sctn-tracking-risks}

Probing whether a native payment app is installed or ready to pay (via
{{PaymentRequest/canMakePayment()}} or {{PaymentRequest/hasEnrolledInstrument()}}
presents potential tracking risks:

- **Fingerprinting**: Websites could query multiple payment methods to silently
    fingerprint the user based on the specific set of installed payment apps.
- **App availability**: In addition to fingerprinting risks, a users installed
    payment apps can leak information about the user to a website. For example,
    the existence of a specific payment app could indicate user geography or
    socio-economic status.
- **Website to app communication**: When determining if an app is [=able to
    handle a payment request=], passing any information from the
    {{PaymentRequest}} call to the app may allow installed payment apps to
    learn information about the user (such as what websites they are visiting)
    without explicit user interaction and consent.

To mitigate these risks:

- User agents MUST NOT perform background readiness checks with native payment
    apps when operating in private browsing modes.

    TODO: We should add text for this somewhere in the spec, but since
          hasEnrolledInstrument is gone from {{PaymentRequest}} there isn't
          currently a place to hang it from.

TODO: Add more requirements, and note where implementations (Chromium) don't
meet them today.

## Payment method verification and spoofing prevention ## {#sctn-spoofing}

User agents MUST NOT allow a [=URL-based payment method identifier=] to claim a
native app that it doesn't own, or allow a native-app to claim a [=URL-based
payment method identifier=] that it doesn't own. To avoid such spoofing:

- User agents MUST only allow [=native payment app providers=] that verify
    native apps against the information present in the [=application
    manifest=], most importantly the package identifier and crptographic
    signing certificate fingerprints.

- Native apps claiming support for a URL-based payment method identifier
    MUST be validated against the [=payment method manifest=] for that
    identifier

